<UPF version="2.0.1">
  <PP_INFO>

 ELECTRON PSEUDOPOTENTIAL (z_valence=-1 variant) — derived from anti-proton-ONCV-1.2.upf
 Modification 2026-06-21: z_valence set to -1.00 (NOT 0.00) so the projectile's
 ionic charge is CONSISTENT with its +1/r repulsive local potential. With
 z_valence=0 the projectile was invisible to the ion-ion Ewald sum (see
 inq-study/src/ionic/interaction.hpp:329, charge[]=valence_charge()), so it felt
 the carbon ELECTRONS (repulsion via local pp) but NOT the carbon NUCLEI
 (attraction) -> spurious vacuum deceleration in graphene. z_valence=-1 restores
 the nuclear attraction (cancels the repulsion at long range for neutral
 graphene) and makes the local-potential G=0/alpha term consistent. The host
 C++ must add .extra_electrons(+1) to keep the quantum electron count at the
 graphene value (96); the cell then carries the physical net -1 charge with a
 +1 uniform compensating background. Jellium was unaffected by z_valence=0
 (no nuclei; uniform medium), which is why the bug surfaced only here.
 Modification 2026-05-07: this is an "electron" pseudopotential —
 same -1 Coulomb tail as anti-proton-ONCV-1.2.upf (both are bare point
 charges of charge -1), but the projectile mass is set to the electron
 mass (5.4857991e-4 amu => 1.0000 atomic units = m_e) at runtime in
 the calling C++ code via inq::ionic::species(sym).mass(amu). The UPF
 format carries no mass attribute, so this rename + comment is for
 human verification only; the actual physics change is host-side.

 Below is the original antiproton header preserved verbatim:
=================================================================
 ANTIPROTON PSEUDOPOTENTIAL — derived from H_ONCV_PBE-1.2.upf
 Modifications applied 2026-05-07:

   1. Changed the PP_HEADER edits:
        z_valence       1.00 -> 0.00
                        (no electrons contributed to KS system)
        number_of_proj  2 -> 0
        is_coulomb      F -> T
                        (declares this as a bare local Coulomb PP)

   2. PP_NONLOCAL block PRESENT BUT EMPTY (with empty PP_DIJ child)
        (no bound electrons to scatter, no KB projectors needed)
        UPDATE 2026-05-08: pseudopod's UPF2 parser
        (inq/external_libs/pseudopod/formats/upf2.hpp:159) null-derefs
        if PP_NONLOCAL is missing, even when number_of_proj=0. So the
        block exists but is empty. The DIJ stub has size=0; the parser
        loop iterates zero times because nprojectors()==0.

   3. This is the main change. PP_LOCAL extended from r = 6.01 -> 50.00 Bohr
        Extension uses the bare -2/r form (Rydberg, e^2 = 2, conversion from Bohr)
        Necessary because the unscreened Coulomb tail is
        long-ranged: at r = 6 Bohr, V = -0.33 Ry, not zero. 
        Essentially, even though the original PP_LOCAL provided unscreened potential, 
        we are changing this because, there is no electron in the system, with this
        ion to screen the charge. Hence, we've extended the range of the grid it is 
        simulated in. This way, we are truly representing the 1/r decay of the coulombic potential

   4. PP_LOCAL sign FLIPPED: V_loc -> -V_loc
        Proton (-1/r, attractive) -> Antiproton (+1/r, repulsive)
        Verified: V_loc * r -> +2.000 Ry at large r

   5. PP_RHOATOM extended with zeros beyond original mesh
        (no valence charge to initialise SCF density with)

   6. PP_R, PP_RAB, mesh_size, rho_cutoff updated for new mesh
        New mesh: 5001 uniform points, dr = 0.01, r_max = 50.0

 Use case: bare antiproton projectile in INQ rt-TDDFT simulations.
 The projectile mass should be set in the INQ input file; this
 UPF carries no mass attribute (mass is host-code-side).
=================================================================

 H_ONCV_PBE-1.2.upf

